---
# Foreign doc attachment (1.9.0 W6): a doc block inside a foreign block attaches to
# the function it precedes and renders in a Foreign Functions section, in markdown and
# in the additive `foreigns` json array with per-function spans. A doc inside the block
# that precedes no function dangles, and a `@param` that names no foreign parameter
# drifts, both rejected by `dusk doc` like everywhere else.
test doc_foreign
mode tool
argv doc examples/doc_foreign.dusk
out # Module doc_foreign\n\nParadigm: procedural\n\nThe doc_foreign module documents the foreign functions it declares. A doc block inside a foreign block attaches to the function it precedes, exactly like a doc before a top-level func, and the block renders in a Foreign Functions section.\n\n## Functions\n\n### main\n\n```dusk\nfunc main() -> int32\n```\n\n## Foreign Functions\n\n### abs\n\n```dusk\nfunc abs(n: int32) -> int32\n```\n\nReturns the absolute value of a 32 bit integer.\n\nParameters:\n\n- `n`: the value to take the magnitude of\n\nReturns: the non negative magnitude of n\n\n### puts\n\n```dusk\nfunc puts(s: *raw char) -> int32\n```\n\nWrites s and a trailing newline to standard output.\n\n### snprintf\n\n```dusk\nfunc snprintf(buf: *raw char, size: int64, fmt: *raw char, ...) -> int32\n```\n\nFormats into a bounded buffer, the variadic case.\n\nParameters:\n\n- `buf`: the destination buffer\n- `size`: the buffer capacity in bytes\n- `fmt`: the printf style format string\n\n### labs\n\n```dusk\nfunc labs(n: int64) -> int64\n```\n
code 0
---
