test shadow_sibling_closure
mode run
file examples/shadow_sibling_closure.dusk
out 7\n42\n
---
# The scope chain composes with the async state machine: an if-block shadow whose
# binding crosses an await is retired at the branch end, so the outer read is
# correct. Frame slots stay distinct; the repair is orthogonal to async.
test shadow_if_async
mode run
file examples/shadow_if_async.dusk
out 2\n1\n
---
# Defer replay resolves the registration environment, the function frame, not a
# shadow that is live at the flushing return. Before the frame-0-only replay the
# defer reprinted the inner "inner"; now the block prints inner and the defer 7.
test defer_shadow
mode run
file examples/defer_shadow.dusk
out inner\n7\n
---
# The frame-0-only defer rule is per-state, so it composes with a lambda's own
# frame: the lambda's defer resolves the lambda frame's n (3), not the if-block's
# shadow (30), when it flushes from inside that block.
test defer_lambda_shadow
mode run
file examples/defer_lambda_shadow.dusk
out 30\n3\n1\n
---
# The lambda capture walk scopes its bound-name set per nested block, so an
# if-block binding of x no longer masks a later `return x` that names the outer x.
# Before the fix this under-captured and died with "unsupported identifier
# expression"; now it captures the outer x and runs.
test lambda_sibling_shadow
mode run
file examples/lambda_sibling_shadow.dusk
out 3\n10\n
---
# The same under-capture, but the masked name also exists as a top-level function:
# before the scoped capture walk the block's int x masked the capture and `x()`
# resolved to the global func x (9); now it captures the outer lambda and calls it.
test lambda_mask_call
mode run
file examples/lambda_mask_call.dusk
out 1\n5\n
---
# A binding that shadows the `using` allocator parameter is refused: bare alloc and
# free resolve the ambient allocator through the parameter name, so rebinding it
# would redirect allocation. The cross-type shadow crashed at runtime before.
test alloc_shadow_fail
mode check_fail
file examples/alloc_shadow_fail.dusk
err_has cannot shadow the allocator parameter
---
# The same reject covers a same-type shadow, which silently drew from the wrong
# arena before rather than crashing.
test alloc_shadow_same_fail
mode check_fail
file examples/alloc_shadow_same_fail.dusk
err_has cannot shadow the allocator parameter
---
# A method honors a `using` allocator like a free function: bare alloc inside the
# method draws from the arena, so its used counter reaches 8. Before the method
# lowering scanned for `using`, alloc fell to the default heap and used stayed 0.
test method_using
mode run
file examples/method_using.dusk
out 8\n
---
# An async func may take a `using` allocator: the parameter rides the task frame and
# bare alloc resolves it by name, so the arena backs the allocation. async_using_await
# proves the allocator is re-read from the frame after a suspension.
test async_using
mode run
file examples/async_using.dusk
out 8\n
---
test async_using_await
mode run
file examples/async_using_await.dusk
out 21\n
---
# A lambda with a `using` allocator is rejected: a lambda body has no ambient
# allocator, so the parameter was a silent no-op before.
test lambda_using_fail
mode check_fail
file examples/lambda_using_fail.dusk
err_has a lambda cannot take a using parameter
---
# A deferred expression may open its own scopes. The defer's match arm binds v and
# is pushed during replay; the replay resolves the scopes the deferred expression
# opens and the function frame, skipping the scopes live at the flush, so the arm
# binder resolves and `A(v) => v + 100` yields 105.
test defer_matcharg
mode run
file examples/defer_matcharg.dusk
out 1\n105\n
---
# A nested lambda's parameter no longer leaks into the enclosing capture walk. The
# inner lambda's parameter n shares the outer local n's name; the scoped capture
# walk confines it, so the outer lambda captures n = 42 and yields 42 + g(1) = 44.
test nested_lambda_pollute
mode run
file examples/nested_lambda_pollute.dusk
out 44\n
---
# Interpolated strings (1.11.1). The lexer, parser, walker sweep, and per-hole
# printability check land first; codegen through the cool_fsb builder family and the
# run-parity goldens follow. fstr_check_ok now runs and prints every legal shape;
# the parity golden pins f"{v}" byte for byte against the println("{}", v) twin; the
# twins prove each illegal hole or malformed literal is rejected with its own
# diagnostic.
test fstr_check_ok
