---
# A field read off a scalar has no member to read and no lowering. It passed check
# clean and died in codegen as an unsupported field expression naming no file and no
# line before; it is a named check error with a span now, reported once at the first
# link of the chain.
test a_field_read_off_a_scalar_is_rejected
mode check_fail
file examples/scalarfield_fail.dusk
err_has cannot access a field of int64; a field access takes a struct, an array or a slice '.len', or an error '.message'
---
# A string carries no fields either, and its reject names the builtin that answers
# the read a program reaches for.
test a_field_read_off_a_string_names_str_len
mode check_fail
file examples/stringfield_fail.dusk
err_has cannot access a field of string; a string has no fields, its byte count is str_len(s)
---
# An index into a scalar has no element to read and no lowering, the same divergence
# on the index side: check clean, codegen dead with no file and no line.
test an_index_into_a_scalar_is_rejected
mode check_fail
file examples/scalarindex_fail.dusk
err_has cannot index int64; an index or a range slice takes an array, a slice, a string, or a pointer
---
# The legal field and index shapes beside those rejects, pinned so neither widens
# onto one: a struct field, a nested chain, a field through a deref, '.len' on an
# array and a slice, an index into an array, a slice, and a string, a range slice of
# an array and of a string, an index through a raw pointer, and an index inside a
# generic body where the element is still a hole on the surface pass.
test the_legal_field_and_index_shapes_run
mode run
file examples/fieldindex_ok.dusk
out 2\n7\n3\n9\n4\n30\n2\n30\n20\nu\ndu\n11\n
---
# A fixed array holds exactly the count its type declares, and an array literal
# builds at its own length with no padding and no truncation, so a literal of any
# other count used to hand clang a differently sized aggregate with no dusk error to
# its name: `mut r: int64[64] = [0]` emitted a `[1 x i64]` store into a `[64 x i64]`
# slot. The count is checked in the one literal fit walk, so the reject lands at
# every position that walk reaches, short and long alike.
test an_array_literal_of_the_wrong_length_is_rejected
mode check_fail
file examples/arrlitlen_fail.dusk
err_has the array literal has 1 element(s) but int64[8] holds 8; a short literal is not padded, write every element
err_has the array literal has 3 element(s) but int64[2] holds 2; drop the extra elements or widen the type
err_has the array literal has 1 element(s) but int64[2] holds 2; a short literal is not padded, write every element
err_has the array literal has 3 element(s) but int64[4] holds 4; a short literal is not padded, write every element
err_has the array literal has 1 element(s) but int64[3] holds 3; a short literal is not padded, write every element
err_has the array literal has 2 element(s) but int64[4] holds 4; a short literal is not padded, write every element
err_has the array literal has 2 element(s) but int64[3] holds 3; a short literal is not padded, write every element
---
# The accept twin: an exact length literal at each of those same positions, beside
# the four shapes the length rule leaves alone, a literal coerced to a slice which
# takes its own length, a char array filled by a string literal which counts bytes
# by its own rule, an array of slices which counts its slices and not their
# elements, and a fixed array bound from another fixed array value, which is not a
# literal at all.
test an_exact_length_array_literal_runs_at_every_position
mode run
file examples/arrlitlen_ok.dusk
out 8\n30\n8\n9\n4\n300\nHello\n200\n24\n
---
# Two enums may declare a variant of the same name whose payloads differ in kind, and
# the escape verdict for one of them must not read the other's payload. The collected
# element here is Opt, whose Some carries a scalar, while Held's Some carries a
# managed pointer. The mint's buried view guard asks whether the element reaches a
# managed pointer, and answering out of a table keyed by variant name alone hands back
# whichever enum registered Some last, which rejects this legal program. The answer
# comes off the enum's own variant slot, so the mint stands, the deep copy survives a
# forced collection, and the scalar payload reads back.
test a_variant_name_shared_with_a_managed_enum_leaves_a_scalar_collect_legal
mode run
file examples/collectorvshadow_ok.dusk
out 7\n
---
# The same program with the two enums declared in the other order. A name keyed read
# is order sensitive, wrong in one order and right in the other by accident, so both
# orders are pinned.
test a_shared_variant_name_leaves_a_scalar_collect_legal_in_either_order
mode run
file examples/collectorvshadowrev_ok.dusk
out 7\n
---
# The reject twin, proving the slot read is precise and not permissive. The collected
# element is the enum whose Some really does carry a pointer to an object holding a
# frame view, and the scalar Some is declared after it, the order in which a name
# keyed read would answer with the scalar and let the mint through.
test a_managed_enum_payload_is_caught_when_a_later_enum_shares_the_variant_name
mode check_fail
file examples/collectorvshadow_fail.dusk
err_has a collected slice element holds a pointer to an object that stores a view of the current frame
---
# The same reject with the two enums declared in the other order, the direction a name
# keyed read answers correctly by accident.
test a_managed_enum_payload_reject_holds_in_either_declaration_order
mode check_fail
file examples/collectorvshadowrev_fail.dusk
err_has a collected slice element holds a pointer to an object that stores a view of the current frame
---
# The folded constant rule computes an expression of bare literals at int64, exactly as
# codegen does, and ranges the result against the target. A fold whose value passes
# int64's top lands on a negative pattern there, and reading that pattern as the value
# refused `9223372036854775807 + 1` into a uint64 that holds it exactly. uint64 is the
# one target whose values are all of int64's patterns, so an overflowing fold is no
# longer a reject there and the stored bits are the value that prints. The last binding
# is the other half of the rule: a fold that wanders out of int64 and back is ranged on
# the value it ends with, which is the value codegen computes.
test a_folded_constant_past_int64s_top_is_the_value_a_uint64_target_holds
mode run
file examples/constover_ok.dusk
out 9223372036854775808\n9223372036854775810\n18446744073709551615\n1\n
---
# The compile fail twin. Every target narrower than uint64 still refuses a fold that
# left int64, and the reject names the overflow instead of the wrapped pattern, since
# that pattern is not the value that was written. The `err_absent` lines pin that: the
# old text named a negative the program never wrote. The uint64 binding proves the
# waiver did not swallow the negative that the fold really does name.
test a_folded_constant_that_overflows_int64_is_refused_by_every_narrower_target
mode check_fail
file examples/constover_fail.dusk
err_has constant expression overflows int64 and does not fit in uint8; cast if the wrap is meant
err_has constant expression overflows int64 and does not fit in int32; cast if the wrap is meant
err_has constant expression overflows int64 and does not fit in uint32; cast if the wrap is meant
err_has constant -9223372036854775808 does not fit in uint64; an unsigned type holds no negative value, cast if the wrap is meant
err_absent constant -9223372036854775808 does not fit in uint8
err_absent constant -9223372036854775808 does not fit in 32 bits
err_absent constant -4 does not fit in uint32
