# A semicolon ends a statement exactly as a newline does, so a trailing one is fine and
# `a = 1; b = 2` is two statements. What is refused is a semicolon with nothing before it
# to end: at the head of a body, doubled after a statement that already closed, and alone
# on a line. Each is named where it sits rather than derailing the expression grammar, so
# the file reports three strays and no "expected an expression".
